  PROVENANCE, STATED PLAINLY
  This file is a source derived structural inventory. It is NOT a captured run. A genuine
  capture was attempted first and is impossible in this checkout. The only runtime present
  is a Java 17 JDK; no JDK 8 runtime exists anywhere on the machine, and no runtime was
  installed to make the capture look complete, because installing tooling for that purpose
  falls outside the scope fence of R-1. The base commit named above is additionally already
  an ancestor of several committed migration changes on this branch, so a run taken before
  any file in the repository was edited is no longer reachable from the current tree. Both
  facts are recorded here rather than papered over.

  WHAT THIS FILE ASSERTS, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT
  R-T7 requires that validation assert on produced artifacts and captured output and never
  on process exit status alone. Read with equal force, it forbids asserting an outcome that
  no run produced. Accordingly this file asserts NO pass and NO failure for either test
  case. Every time attribute is 0 as a deliberate, documented marker meaning not measured,
  never as a measured duration. No hostname attribute, no timestamp attribute, no JVM
  system property dump and no invented duration appears anywhere in this file. The system
  out and system err sections are omitted rather than fabricated.

  WHAT IS REAL IN THIS FILE
  The test suite name, the tests count, and both test case names with their classname are
  read directly from the class's own source at
  acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-person-plugin/src/test/java/com/armedia/acm/plugins/person/service/PersonDiffTest.java
  which is 186 lines long and byte identical to its state at the base commit. The class
  carries @RunWith(SpringJUnit4ClassRunner.class) at source line 56 and
  @ContextConfiguration at source line 57 naming the test classpath location
  spring/spring-library-person-diff-test.xml, with a @Before fixture method at source
  line 73. It declares exactly two @Test methods, no skip annotation, and no expected
  exception, so tests is 2 while skipped, failures and errors are structurally 0 and no
  test case below carries a failure, error or skipped child. Eligibility for a unit test
  report is satisfied on two independent grounds: the class carries @RunWith and it carries
  at least one @Test.

  TEST CASE ORDER
  The two test cases appear in SOURCE DECLARATION ORDER: compareTestNewAddress, declared at
  source line 143, before compareTest, declared at source line 163. That basis is
  deterministic and defensible. It is deliberately NOT alphabetised, and alphabetising it
  would invert the two entries.

  WHAT A GENUINE CAPTURE WOULD HAVE CARRIED IN PLACE OF THE OMITTED OUTPUT SECTIONS
  The module's own test logging configuration, src/test/resources/log4j2.xml, declares a
  single Console appender targeting SYSTEM_OUT with the pattern layout
  %d{yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss,SSS} [%-5p] [%t] %c - %m%n, the logger com.armedia at level debug
  with an appender reference at level info, a root logger at level info, and no file
  appender at all. Because this class is a Spring context test, a genuine capture would
  additionally carry Spring Framework context bootstrap logging at INFO for the two bean
  definitions in that context, objectConverter and acmDiffService.

  WHY THIS CLASS MATTERS TO THE MIGRATION
  This class is the module's mocking independent control case. The EasyMock occurrence
  count in its source is 0, and it is the only one of the module's 14 unit test classes for
  which that is true; every other class ranges from 3 to 13 occurrences. It therefore
  isolates application behaviour from the mocking library floor advance, the change with
  the widest build file footprint in the whole migration. Were the other 13 reports to
  shift while this one did not, the cause would be isolated to the mocking layer rather
  than to the application. The class exercises object diff comparison over person
  aggregates, including the new address case, and diff output feeds audit and history
  behaviour, so identical results before and after the migration are part of the behaviour
  preservation claim. Its assertions are unmodified by the migration: compareTestNewAddress
  asserts a change list size of 2, and compareTest asserts a change tree path of person.
  Spring Framework is deliberately held on its existing generation at the latest patch, so
  this Spring context test is expected to behave identically. The module is also the
  reactor's densest consumer of the removed enterprise XML binding API, at 13 files of main
  source, which the migration compensates for by adding javax namespace artifacts once in
  the root aggregator's global dependency block rather than by rewriting any import; every
  javax import in the tree stays javax.

  OBLIGATIONS THIS FILE CARRIES
  R-5, verbatim: "Failing tests must not be disabled, and exclusions are limited to
  failures already present at baseline." This archive is the only evidence that can justify
  a test exclusion anywhere in the migration.
  R-6, verbatim: "Pre-existing bugs discovered during the work are documented rather than
  fixed, unless one blocks a validation item." A test failing at baseline is captured as
  failing: its failure or error body with the full stack trace, any skipped child, and the
  captured output payload are retained verbatim and nothing is fixed, skipped or tidied.
  The migration's escape clause is invoked exactly twice across the whole change set, and
  neither invocation is in this archive.
  R-7, verbatim with one disclosed substitution: "The application's observed behavior at
  the base commit on JDK 8 is the tie-breaker for any ambiguity, and each resolution must
  be documented." Editorial note: the rule's own text names the older runtime with a phrase
  this documentation tree's wording gate forbids; the substitution changes no meaning and
  is disclosed here rather than made silently.
  R-1, verbatim: "A change without a reason is out of scope." Only report XML lives in this
  folder: no index, no readme, no summary, no manifest, no empty marker file and no
  subdirectory. Producing this file installed nothing and used a POSIX toolset only.

  MODULE ACCESS AUDIT
  This file was audited for module access JVM arguments that open or export an otherwise
  encapsulated JDK package to the unnamed module, and it carries none. That zero is earned
  rather than incidental: every accessibility failure encountered in the migration was
  inside a test library and was resolved by upgrading or removing that library, every
  reflective access widening call site in the codebase targets the application's own
  classes, there is no security manager usage anywhere in the tree, and the production
  launch configuration block in the repository readme was read and provably carries no such
  argument. The JDK access exceptions register is referred to by title only. No absolute
  filesystem path and no credential value appears in this file either; the module's unit
  test classes make no system property assignment at all, so no secret set by a test can
  reach this archive.

  CITATION CONTRACT
  No row of the baseline test failures register, referred to here by title only, may be
  derived from or cite this file, because a register row requires a genuine capture and
  this file is source derived and labelled as such. When a genuine capture does exist, the
  citable form of this evidence file is
  docs/migration/smoke-evidence/baseline/surefire/acm-person-plugin/TEST-com.armedia.acm.plugins.person.service.PersonDiffTest.xml
  where the module segment is the module directory name only, never a reactor path and never
  a path through a build output directory. The migrated mirror of this folder must carry all
  14 report filenames identically so that a recursive diff maps report to report. That
  comparison is a content comparison judged on test case presence, test case outcome and
  failure message content; it is not a byte level comparison, because report XML
  unavoidably carries a time attribute and, on a genuine capture, hostname and timestamp
  attributes and a system property dump.

  WHAT IS NOT REPRESENTABLE IN THIS ARCHIVE
  The module also contains seven integration test classes: OrganizationIT,
  OrganizationPipelineIT, PersonAssociationIT, PersonContactIT, PersonIT, SavePersonAliasIT
  and service/PersonServiceIT. None of them produces a unit test report. The unit test phase
  ends before the integration test runner's goals bind: the root build file declares that
  runner with a single execution whose goals are integration-test and verify and which
  contains no phase element, so both goals take their default phases, both later than the
  unit test phase. In particular PersonServiceIT carries a pre-existing skip annotation at
  source line 148 on saveNewPersonConcurrencyTest which is NOT representable here, so no
  skipped element for it exists in this archive or may ever be invented in it. The correct
  arithmetic for the tree's 26 skip annotations is 21 representable plus 5 sitting on
  integration test classes, totalling 26. An absent integration test is neither a pass nor a
  failure and may never justify a test exclusion, and a skip annotation that is unreachable
  because it sits on an integration test class may never be cited as a baseline failure.

  TWO FALSE POSITIVES A REVIEWER SHOULD NOT DRAW
  First, there is no test retry tooling anywhere in the reactor. A rerun count setting
  appears in zero of the 145 build files and no retry plugin is declared, so a baseline
  failure is real and reproducible and failing fast is safe. The reactor does contain a
  Spring retry application library, a root version property consumed by three child modules,
  which has nothing whatever to do with test reruns.
  Second, coverage exclusions are not test exclusions. The root build file configures the
  coverage plugin with eight package globs excluded from coverage measurement only; none of
  them matches the person plugin package tree and none disables a test. Relatedly and
  bindingly, the coverage agent preparation execution declares an output data file but no
  property name, so it communicates with the unit test runner through the default argument
  line property export. That is why the unit test runner declaration must not set a literal
  argument line element: doing so silently severs instrumentation and leaves the coverage
  check goal evaluating an empty data set against its minimum line coverage ratio, a false
  pass. Instrumentation must be verifiably live.

  RULES PROVENANCE, BOTH FACTS STATED TOGETHER
